# Copy to `.env.local` and fill in what you need:
#
#     cp .env.example .env.local
#
# `.env.local` is the only env file anything loads — see src/env.ts. `.env.prod`
# exists as a record of what production needs and is read by nothing.
#
# **This file beats what the shell exported**, which is the opposite of the usual
# rule and is deliberate — src/env.ts says why, and this comment claimed the
# reverse until 2026-09-02. So `LOG_LEVEL=debug npm run dev` works only while no
# `LOG_LEVEL` line is set below; if one is, the file wins and the command looks
# like it did nothing.
#
# This file is the exception to `.env*` in .gitignore, so it must never hold a
# real value. Names and comments only.
#
# docs/project/setup-dev.md — the commands these feed
# docs/project/supabase-local.md — the database half

# ── Model calls ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# **Every model call in the app.** Since 2026-08-27 there are no exceptions: the
# pipeline as well as explain, chat, search, PDF reading and embeddings. Without
# it nothing can be ingested at all, and a reader who selects a passage or sends
# a chat message gets an error back in place of an answer. The reading view
# itself still renders. https://openrouter.ai/keys
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# **OPTIONAL, and one eval is the whole of what wants it. Leave it blank.**
#
# Nothing in `src/` has read it since 2026-08-27, when the seven pipeline stages
# started reaching the same models through OpenRouter's Anthropic-compatible
# endpoint (docs/project/ai-gateway.md), and no deployment wants it — it is not
# in the health report. The last caller in the repo is the PDF bake-off's four
# `transport: "anthropic"` arms, which exist to compare talking to Anthropic
# directly against going through OpenRouter, and cannot be moved onto OpenRouter
# without becoming a comparison of OpenRouter with itself
# (`bakeoff-anthropic-transport`, src/spend-declarations.ts). Blank, the
# bake-off skips those four and says so; the other seven arms run.
#
# It is a second vendor, a second bill and a second key to rotate, so
# `tests/no-undeclared-spend.test.ts` fails if a second caller appears.
# https://console.anthropic.com/settings/keys
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# **A SECOND, SEPARATE BILL — and the one spend nothing here can total.**
#
# Live conversation mode (docs/project/live-conversation.md) talks to OpenAI
# directly, because OpenRouter has no realtime API to route to. So this is a
# different account from OPENROUTER_API_KEY, it is **not covered by the spend cap
# set on the OpenRouter account**, and `npm run cost` cannot see a penny of it:
# the audio is a WebRTC connection from the reader's browser straight to OpenAI,
# so no ai_calls row is ever written. The cap for it has to be set in the OpenAI
# dashboard, which is Greg's action rather than anything code can do.
#
# Roughly $0.06-$0.46 a minute on gpt-realtime-2.1, capped at 20 minutes a
# session and uncapped in sessions per reader. src/live.ts is the only file that
# reads it. Blank, the Talk button refuses to start and nothing else notices.
#
# `evals/live/*.mts` spend the same key, and jargon-recovery.mts also buys
# text-to-speech from /v1/audio/speech. Both are named in UNMETERED_SPEND in
# src/spend-declarations.ts, which `npm run cost` prints every run.
# https://platform.openai.com/api-keys
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# ── The local database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `npm run db:start` prints all of these and `npm run db:status` reprints them.
# They are the Supabase CLI's fixed local values — the same on every machine, so
# they are not secrets, and they are worth pasting rather than inventing.

# What scripts/db-migrate.ts and everything Drizzle connects to. Port 54362 is
# the database itself; 54361 is the API gateway in front of it.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@127.0.0.1:54362/postgres

# For Supabase Auth, once there is a gate to authenticate against. `npm run
# db:seed-owner` needs the URL and the service-role key; nothing else reads them.
SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54361
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Signing in. docs/project/auth.md.
#
# The two VITE_ ones are compiled into the BROWSER BUNDLE at build time, so they
# are public by construction — only ever the publishable key here, never
# sb_secret_ and never the service-role key. tests/no-secrets-in-bundle.test.ts
# checks, and it decodes JWT payloads rather than grepping for the word, because
# a legacy service-role key does not spell "service_role" in the clear.
#
# `npx supabase status` prints the local values. In production these are set on
# the Vercel project, and BEFORE the build that needs them — setting them after
# a deploy changes nothing until the next one.
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54361
VITE_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=
# The server verifies tokens with this. No privilege needed: it fetches a public
# key set and does arithmetic. Falls back to SUPABASE_ANON_KEY if unset.
SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=

# Google sign-in, read by the local Supabase stack (supabase/config.toml).
# Shared with the old app's OAuth client — docs/plans/auth-supabase.md.
SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
SUPABASE_AUTH_EXTERNAL_GOOGLE_SECRET=

# ── Payments (Stripe) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The TEST-MODE secret key (`sk_test_…`), from
# https://dashboard.stripe.com/test/apikeys. Everything billing-related reads
# it; blank, billing routes refuse and nothing else notices. It is on
# `gjd-remote push-env`'s allowlist so dev boxes inherit it — and push-env
# refuses a live-mode key (`sk_live_…`) by its prefix, so the live key never
# reaches a shared box: it belongs only in Vercel's production env.
# docs/plans/260902i-stripe-payments-and-subscription-tiers.md
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# NOT on the push-env allowlist, deliberately: locally this is minted per
# machine by `stripe listen`, so the laptop's value would be wrong on the box.
# In production it is the dashboard webhook endpoint's signing secret, set on
# Vercel.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Where Stripe sends a reader back to after Checkout or the Portal. Leave it
# blank almost always: production uses PUBLIC_ORIGIN (src/urls.ts) and reads no
# variable at all, a Vercel preview uses its own VERCEL_URL, and a dev server on
# 5273 is the fallback. Set it only for a **worktree's** dev server, which lands
# on 5274, 5275… — otherwise Stripe returns you to somebody else's checkout.
# src/billing/checkout.ts § billingReturnOrigin.
SPIDERYARN_BASE_URL=

# NOTE: there is no STRIPE_PRICE_* variable, deliberately. Tiers and their
# Stripe price ids live in the `billing_tiers` table since 2026-09-02, so they
# can be changed without a deploy and without pasting an id onto three machines.
# `npx tsx scripts/stripe-setup.ts --apply` reads the rows, makes Stripe match,
# and writes the price id back. docs/project/billing.md.

# ── Optional ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# A personal access token from https://supabase.com/dashboard/account/tokens,
# needed only by scripts/supabase-auth-config.ts, which reads and writes the
# REMOTE project's auth settings. It is a management credential — it can create
# and delete projects — so leave it unset unless you are using that script.
# docs/plans/google-sign-in-production.md
SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# `debug` to see everything, `silent` to see nothing. Defaults sensibly per
# environment; the useful case is one failing test: LOG_LEVEL=debug npx vitest…
# docs/project/logging.md
LOG_LEVEL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- monitoring --
# Error tracking. Every one of these may be left blank, and blank is what a
# laptop should have: with no DSN the SDK is never initialised, nothing leaves
# the machine, and no test needs a network stub.
# docs/plans/error-monitoring-sentry.md

# The server half. Read at RUN time, so setting it takes effect on the next
# request.
SENTRY_DSN=

# The browser half — the same DSN, under Vite's prefix, because only `VITE_*`
# reaches client code. **Read at BUILD time and compiled into the bundle**, so
# setting it on the Vercel project changes nothing until the next build. Same
# trap as VITE_SUPABASE_URL; see docs/project/deployment.md.
VITE_SENTRY_DSN=

# Source-map upload, at BUILD time only. All three or none: with any of them
# missing the plugin is not added at all, and the build then deliberately emits
# no source maps either — so a build without a token ships no maps rather than
# publishing this project's source in `dist/`. The token is a real secret and
# needs "Project: Read & Write" and "Release: Admin".
# scripts/sentry-build.ts
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
SENTRY_ORG=
SENTRY_PROJECT=

# Override the app-wide model for one surface. The default for both is the single
# constant in src/models.ts, which is the thing to change if you want to move all
# of it at once.
SPIDERYARN_EXPLAIN_MODEL=
SPIDERYARN_CHAT_MODEL=

# Path to a CA certificate for the database connection. Only for a remote; there
# is a committed default, and locally there is no TLS to verify.
PGSSLROOTCERT=

# Who owns rows written OUTSIDE a request — the CLI and the pipeline.
# Inside a request it is always the signed-in user and this gets no vote
# (src/owner.ts). Unset, it is the seeded row-owner `dev@spideryarn.local`,
# which nobody signs in as — so the library you see after signing in is empty
# however much has been ingested.
#
# Set it to the administrator's id from src/admin.ts. That puts CLI work on the
# shelf you actually look at, and it is on `gjd-remote push-env`'s allowlist, so a
# new box is right from its first ingest and needs nothing else.
#
# On a database that ALREADY has rows, move them first — `npx tsx
# scripts/db-reown.ts --apply`, which is a dry run without the flag. Setting this
# on top of rows owned by somebody else is what turned eight test files red when
# it was measured on 2026-08-31: the shelf reads empty and globally unique slugs
# report your own articles as another reader's.
# docs/project/supabase-local.md § One shelf, and how to get there
SPIDERYARN_OWNER_ID=

# Which store serves article reads: `postgres` or `files`. `npm run dev` itself
# now defaults to `postgres` (package.json, since 2026-09-02); everything else —
# a CLI script, a test, `vite` run some other way — still defaults to `files`,
# and that default is the wrong one for new work, which includes your laptop
# (CLAUDE.md, docs/project/database.md).
#
# Setting it here keeps every process agreeing. Left unset, a CLI script such as
# `npm run db:seed-dev` still writes seeded rows without the dev server necessarily
# reading `files` too — but anything that does read `files` (set explicitly, or
# started outside `npm run dev`) will show an empty shelf however much has been
# seeded, and *nothing looks wrong*. `npm run db:seed-dev` exits non-zero rather
# than let `npm run setup` report completion over that.
#
# It is read once at module load, so changing it needs the server restarted, and
# it is on `gjd-remote push-env`'s allowlist, so a box inherits it from here.
SPIDERYARN_STORE=postgres

# Only changes which address the seeded row-owner is created with. Nothing signs
# in as it, so this is for telling one seeded row from another in Studio.
SPIDERYARN_OWNER_EMAIL=

# How many database connections this process may hold. Defaults to 5. Small on
# purpose: Supabase's pooler limit is global, and serverless multiplies
# instances, so a big number here is refused connections somewhere else.
DATABASE_POOL_MAX=

# NOT a variable to set here, and listed only so nobody adds it: db-migrate.ts
# refuses any non-localhost DATABASE_URL unless DB_MIGRATE_ALLOW_REMOTE=yes is
# set as well. Type it on the command line for the one migration that needs it.
# Putting it in a file removes the only thing standing between a stray
# connection string and a migrated production database.

# Pay-as-you-go auth for the Codex CLI, used by scripts/run-codex.ts to dispatch a GPT/Codex
# subagent — see docs/reusable/codex-cli-as-subagent.md. Optional: `codex login` against a ChatGPT
# subscription works too, and this takes precedence over it, so it is also the fallback for when
# that subscription runs out of credits. Not OPENAI_API_KEY: `codex exec` does not read that one.
CODEX_API_KEY=

# The origins that are *us*, comma-separated, for the sanitiser rule that stops
# an article's own HTML addressing our API (src/sanitize-policy.ts, and
# docs/project/security.md § An article may not address our own API). Almost
# always leave this blank: on Vercel the deployment host comes from
# VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL and VERCEL_URL, which are set for us, and in dev
# the localhost fallback covers it. Set it only for a custom domain. It is
# listed here because for two days it was the ONLY source of that host, nobody
# had set it, and the server half of the rule quietly did nothing.
SPIDERYARN_ORIGINS=
